WI-pozut. When auto-pr merges and its post-merge pull then aborts because
pending `.ops` collide with the same paths in the incoming commit, the way out
was three commands: `tracker sync`, a raw fast-forward, `tracker recover`. Two
tracker commands with a plain VCS command between them, none of which reports
whether the next is still needed.

That recipe was documented, short, and still lost to hand-rolled file surgery
the first time it was needed under pressure — by an operator who had just quoted
it correctly. A procedure that loses to improvisation at the moment it matters is
a procedure with a missing command, so this adds the command.

WHAT IT DOES, each step skipped when it is not needed:

  1. flush   pending ops upstream via do_sync
  2. ff      fast-forward now that the collision is gone
  3. restore union-restore anything the journal holds that the tree lacks

Every step reports what it did OR why it did nothing. A silent step is
indistinguishable from a step that was not needed, and that ambiguity is exactly
what sends the next reader back to doing it by hand.

DESIGN POINTS THAT ARE NOT INCIDENTAL:

- NO SYNTHETIC MUTATION. An earlier sketch proposed tripping the flush with a
  canned harmless op repeated every time. Rejected on reading the code: do_sync
  takes no threshold argument (the line-count gate lives in its caller), so there
  is nothing to trick; a mutation writes a real op into a real item's history,
  and the append-only log's whole value is that every line means something; and
  "the same every time" is the worst variant, because op ids are content-hashed
  with skip-existing idempotency, so a byte-identical mutation may add no lines
  and fail to trip the gate at all.

- REFUSES UNDER .git/PR_PENDING. An in-flight auto-pr moves the ops dirs around
  its own rebase; syncing or fast-forwarding underneath it is how the two end up
  fighting over the same files.

- STOPS RATHER THAN FAST-FORWARDING WHEN THE FLUSH FAILS, propagating do_sync's
  exit code. Advancing on top of ops that never reached the remote is precisely
  the collision this exists to prevent.

- DOES NOT SET `tracker-recover-disabled`. do_sync sets it around its own fetch
  on purpose. Here the opposite is wanted: the post-checkout and
  reference-transaction hooks should be LIVE, because restoring ops the pull
  disturbs is their job.

- CLEAN NO-OP on a healthy repo, so it is safe to run reflexively after every
  auto-pr — which is the only way it gets run at all.

DEPRECATION. `recover` stays: it is the primitive `reconcile` calls and the
hooks' entry point. It now prints a notice pointing at `reconcile`, and the exit
code is unchanged. Verified rather than assumed: both `.githooks/post-checkout`
and `.githooks/reference-transaction` invoke it with `>/dev/null 2>&1`, so the
notice is silent on every automatic invocation and surfaces only for manual use.
Had they not redirected, every ref transaction and every checkout would have
begun printing a deprecation warning.

13 new tests, and the 535 existing tracker tests unchanged and green. Confirmed
against a real repository: `reconcile --dry-run` reports all three steps, and the
real run correctly declines to claim success when the branch has no upstream,
printing the VCS's own words instead.

AGENTS.md, under explicit owner approval for the governance edit: its Op
Durability bullet named `recover` four times as the thing you run by hand. Those
now name `reconcile`, plus a new sentence saying outright that `reconcile` is the
answer to the aborted-pull state and that the three-step recipe is superseded —
its first step obsolete regardless, since do_sync takes no threshold. Three
`recover` mentions deliberately remain: what the hooks actually call, the
deprecation note, and the description of the superseded recipe.

The two `recover` mentions in the root CHANGELOG are historical released entries
and are deliberately left alone.

